WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.146 --> 00:00:01.300
- Good morning Believe Nation,

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.133
today's message is "learn it all".

00:00:05.524 --> 00:00:07.357
Over to you Tony Hawk.

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:10.714
(basketball bouncing)

00:00:10.714 --> 00:00:12.709
(rooster crowing)

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:15.411
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:15.411 --> 00:00:19.699
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:23.435
- What is it that makes you
a real special champion?

00:00:23.435 --> 00:00:25.627
- I just, I never, well
for one I never quit.

00:00:25.627 --> 00:00:28.649
I never stopped skating,
I never took a hiatus.

00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:30.764
Say, five years and came back
like oh, skating's hot now

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:32.870
I'm going to get back into it.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.120
So I have that consistency, but I think

00:00:37.276 --> 00:00:41.094
that for me I really like
learning new techniques.

00:00:41.094 --> 00:00:43.101
And a lot of people if they're skating

00:00:43.101 --> 00:00:45.648
they stick with this one style of skating,

00:00:45.648 --> 00:00:49.427
this one set of tricks and
don't want to branch out

00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:50.843
because they're afraid they won't

00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:52.531
look cool doing it or whatever.

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:55.159
I always wanted to try
the different techniques,

00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:58.723
even if I stumbled on them
I knew that in the end

00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:00.702
it would help me be more well rounded

00:01:00.702 --> 00:01:02.668
and I think that's probably what it is

00:01:02.668 --> 00:01:06.237
is that I wanted to try to learn it all.

00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:07.294
- I think really important to understand

00:01:07.294 --> 00:01:08.833
where innovation comes from.

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.624
If you're an entrepreneur
you want to come up

00:01:10.624 --> 00:01:11.702
with these new innovative ideas.

00:01:11.702 --> 00:01:13.323
You want to break through in your market,

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:14.293
have something that's really different,

00:01:14.293 --> 00:01:17.391
understand where innovation
comes from because

00:01:17.391 --> 00:01:20.503
most of the time
innovation just comes from

00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:23.894
connecting the dots that other
people haven't seen before.

00:01:23.894 --> 00:01:25.814
By looking at different industries,

00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:28.737
different businesses, different cultures,

00:01:28.737 --> 00:01:30.975
and seeing how things work there,

00:01:30.975 --> 00:01:33.484
way over here that
nobody's thought of before,

00:01:33.484 --> 00:01:34.960
not even on camera,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.464
and bringing it back to what you're doing.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:40.382
Steve Jobs famously came up with fonts

00:01:40.382 --> 00:01:42.420
in the personal computing industry

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.749
and that's what helped sell
his first line of computers.

00:01:45.749 --> 00:01:48.381
But Steve Jobs wasn't the first
person to think about fonts,

00:01:48.381 --> 00:01:51.479
and the importance of
writing and writing style,

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:52.768
and the impact that that has.

00:01:52.768 --> 00:01:56.935
He just took a well known
science that people acknowledged

00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.545
the importance and relevancy
of it and just brought it

00:02:00.545 --> 00:02:02.712
to this new field of computers over here.

00:02:02.712 --> 00:02:04.159
He just lined up those two things

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:06.532
that nobody had connected
those dots before.

00:02:06.532 --> 00:02:07.647
That's what you need to do.

00:02:07.647 --> 00:02:10.031
This is where you have the ability

00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:13.399
to really stand out from
everybody else around you.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:16.802
By connecting the dots of
the things that you love

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:20.128
that nobody else has those
combinations of love.

00:02:20.128 --> 00:02:21.647
I love business.

00:02:21.647 --> 00:02:23.326
I love YouTube.

00:02:23.326 --> 00:02:24.928
I love League of Legends.

00:02:24.928 --> 00:02:27.856
I love the Toronto Bluejays, and baseball.

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.464
I love salsa dancing.

00:02:29.464 --> 00:02:31.420
I combined all of these things

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.107
into a business that
nobody else can replicate.

00:02:35.107 --> 00:02:37.544
It's why you can't out-Evan, Evan.

00:02:37.544 --> 00:02:38.886
It's why when people just copy me

00:02:38.886 --> 00:02:41.641
for exactly what I'm doing,
they're never going to win.

00:02:41.641 --> 00:02:42.801
They're not going to innovate.

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:43.830
They're just copy cats.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.938
I don't care about the copy,

00:02:44.938 --> 00:02:46.006
I don't look behind.

00:02:46.006 --> 00:02:48.392
I don't care about blocking
the copy cat people.

00:02:48.392 --> 00:02:51.798
I'm looking forward, and
you need to do the same.

00:02:51.798 --> 00:02:54.766
All of your interests,
all of your passions,

00:02:54.766 --> 00:02:56.578
everything that you've ever liked,

00:02:56.578 --> 00:03:00.189
you can combine all those ideas to make

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:04.356
an amazing, unique, different,
special, valuable business.

00:03:05.945 --> 00:03:07.424
When I took over Toronto Dance Salsa

00:03:07.424 --> 00:03:09.266
at the beginning of
this year as the owner,

00:03:09.266 --> 00:03:12.016
I brought a whole bunch of
things to the salsa industry

00:03:12.016 --> 00:03:14.642
that has never been seen before.

00:03:14.642 --> 00:03:17.857
I'm bringing aspects of
YouTube and entrepreneurship

00:03:17.857 --> 00:03:20.013
and baseball and League of Legends,

00:03:20.013 --> 00:03:21.844
and all the things that I care about,

00:03:21.844 --> 00:03:25.801
into this business that
nobody else can replicate.

00:03:25.801 --> 00:03:27.670
'Cause they don't have the same passions.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.267
And if they're just trying to
copy me they're going to fail.

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:33.377
The way for them to win is to
go more in on their strengths

00:03:33.377 --> 00:03:35.590
and the things that they care about

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.800
and find a way to integrate
that into their business.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.645
This is how you are going
to win as an entrepreneur.

00:03:40.645 --> 00:03:43.589
Stop trying to copy everybody else.

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:48.142
Find the connective tissue
between seemingly unrelated

00:03:48.142 --> 00:03:49.631
industries, hobbies, passions,

00:03:49.631 --> 00:03:52.518
and find a way to make it work.

00:03:52.518 --> 00:03:54.505
Combine the genius of the and.

00:03:54.505 --> 00:03:56.284
That's where you're going to have success.

00:03:56.284 --> 00:03:57.225
Do it now!

00:03:57.225 --> 00:03:58.893
Think about what you love to do,

00:03:58.893 --> 00:04:00.451
what your hobbies are,
what your passions are,

00:04:00.451 --> 00:04:03.489
that people might think is
weird or different or odd.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:04.958
How you spend your spare time,

00:04:04.958 --> 00:04:07.383
where you like to go on vacation.

00:04:07.383 --> 00:04:09.539
What can you pull from all of those things

00:04:09.539 --> 00:04:12.820
to instantly make your business better?

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.090
Figure that out.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.440
You've got some magic ingredients
for an amazing business.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.047
So the questions of the day today is

00:04:19.047 --> 00:04:20.389
I'm curious, what have you learned

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:21.736
from a different industry

00:04:21.736 --> 00:04:23.721
that seemed totally irrelevant,

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:25.372
but then you applied it to your business

00:04:25.372 --> 00:04:26.315
and it worked?

00:04:26.315 --> 00:04:28.653
Super curious to find out,
leave it in the comments below,

00:04:28.653 --> 00:04:30.281
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:04:30.281 --> 00:04:32.574
I also want to gie a quick
shoutout to Brittany Robinson

00:04:32.574 --> 00:04:35.138
from treehousedreampublishing.com.

00:04:35.138 --> 00:04:37.362
Brittany thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:04:37.362 --> 00:04:40.451
Your One Word, it really,
really, really means a lot to me.

00:04:40.451 --> 00:04:42.036
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:04:42.036 --> 00:04:43.356
I believe in you.

00:04:43.356 --> 00:04:45.482
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself.

00:04:45.482 --> 00:04:48.491
And whatever your one word is, much love,

00:04:48.491 --> 00:04:50.292
and I'll see your tomorrow
morning for another

00:04:50.292 --> 00:04:52.685
shot of Entspresso.

00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:54.570
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:58.555
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'

00:04:58.555 --> 00:04:59.811
Hey Believe Nation, we're also looking at

00:04:59.811 --> 00:05:02.411
making a top ten of Tony Hawk.

00:05:02.411 --> 00:05:04.126
Here's a quick sneak peak of some of

00:05:04.126 --> 00:05:05.565
the clips we're thinking of including.

00:05:05.565 --> 00:05:07.792
I hope you enjoy, let
me know what you think.

00:05:07.792 --> 00:05:09.267
- When I started our company, Birdhouse,

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:12.587
in the first three years in
we were ready to give in.

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:14.859
You know, the sales just weren't there,

00:05:14.859 --> 00:05:17.119
the interest wasn't there.

00:05:17.119 --> 00:05:19.046
And same with my skating,

00:05:19.046 --> 00:05:21.332
like I was still improving my skills

00:05:21.332 --> 00:05:23.323
but for no audience.

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:24.970
So it was definitely difficult,

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:28.186
but we embraced those challenges too,

00:05:28.186 --> 00:05:29.820
and especially as athletes,

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.153
I mean, someone poses a challenge to you

00:05:34.094 --> 00:05:36.361
and you have to figure
out how to get over it.

00:05:36.361 --> 00:05:40.367
And so in doing that in my
skating and also in the business

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.915
it was like, well this
is what's coming at us

00:05:42.915 --> 00:05:44.342
we've got to figure out
how to navigate this

00:05:44.342 --> 00:05:46.713
and how to succeed, and a
lot of people just give up.

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:49.020
And especially in skating, I mean,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.927
that's the first sign of someone

00:05:50.927 --> 00:05:52.152
that's going to be successful.

00:05:52.152 --> 00:05:56.129
No matter how far, no matter
how successful they get,

00:05:56.129 --> 00:05:57.078
especially in competition,

00:05:57.078 --> 00:05:58.543
they continue to challenge themselves

00:05:58.543 --> 00:06:00.481
because they want to improve.

00:06:00.481 --> 00:06:01.974
Those are the guys that make it

00:06:01.974 --> 00:06:03.419
way further than anyone else.

00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:05.072
- When you're coming up with a new idea

00:06:05.072 --> 00:06:07.899
for your sport, what is it that you,

00:06:07.899 --> 00:06:09.520
how do you get into the zone,

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.122
then translate that
into the business world?

00:06:13.122 --> 00:06:16.069
How do you come up with that next step,

00:06:16.069 --> 00:06:17.887
the next innovation
that's going to put you

00:06:17.887 --> 00:06:20.304
once again out in front of everybody else?

00:06:20.304 --> 00:06:23.371
- I think it's having the confidence.

00:06:23.371 --> 00:06:26.725
When I approach something,
say just a new trick,

00:06:26.725 --> 00:06:28.922
I know I have the skills,

00:06:28.922 --> 00:06:30.354
the inherent skills to do it.

00:06:30.354 --> 00:06:32.448
I know that I visualized
it and I know I can do it.

00:06:32.448 --> 00:06:34.252
Same goes for business, you know,

00:06:34.252 --> 00:06:36.322
I know, like we did a tour,

00:06:36.322 --> 00:06:38.576
it's called "Boom Boom HuckJam",

00:06:38.576 --> 00:06:40.396
Karl Harris was a big part of that,

00:06:40.396 --> 00:06:42.335
he's here in the audience.

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:46.357
We did a tour, which was
skating, BMX, motocross.

00:06:46.357 --> 00:06:47.640
I visualized the whole thing.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.156
I said you know, we have,
our sports are popular enough

00:06:51.156 --> 00:06:54.140
that we don't have to be a side
show to Warped Tour anymore.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.926
We don't have to be the half
time show to the football game.

00:06:56.926 --> 00:06:59.009
We can get our own crowd.

00:07:00.095 --> 00:07:02.386
We can be the headliner of an arena tour.

00:07:02.386 --> 00:07:06.553
And I designed the ramps
on, stupid me, on a napkin.

00:07:08.134 --> 00:07:09.088
Cost a million bucks.

00:07:09.088 --> 00:07:11.240
(audience laughs)

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.784
And it filled the arena floor.

00:07:13.784 --> 00:07:16.231
It included motocross, skateboard and BMX.

00:07:16.231 --> 00:07:18.688
I just knew that that would work

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:20.566
and I knew that there
were enough kids out there

00:07:20.566 --> 00:07:22.925
that would love to come see a show.

00:07:22.925 --> 00:07:24.828
It was our sports as entertainment,

00:07:24.828 --> 00:07:26.083
it wasn't about competition.

00:07:26.083 --> 00:07:27.660
It was actually my transition from

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:31.642
being a competitor to being a performer.

00:07:31.642 --> 00:07:35.475
And I never thought what
if this doesn't work?

00:07:36.384 --> 00:07:37.217
What if it sucks?

00:07:37.217 --> 00:07:38.087
What if no one comes?

00:07:38.087 --> 00:07:39.197
Like, that's what kills you,

00:07:39.197 --> 00:07:40.609
because you live in that world of doubt

00:07:40.609 --> 00:07:42.420
and it comes to fruition.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.425
And you can't think that way,

00:07:44.425 --> 00:07:47.089
just if you know it's
something that you love

00:07:47.089 --> 00:07:49.107
and you instinctually
think it's going to work

00:07:49.107 --> 00:07:51.622
then you have to go at
it and say it will work,

00:07:51.622 --> 00:07:53.631
and see it through.

00:07:53.631 --> 00:07:55.417
And sometimes it takes years.

00:07:55.417 --> 00:07:57.935
I get so busy I forget to stop and say

00:07:57.935 --> 00:07:59.882
wow I'm actually living the dream.

00:07:59.882 --> 00:08:02.517
And I think that for all of you guys,

00:08:02.517 --> 00:08:06.086
going to college and
aspiring to do something,

00:08:06.086 --> 00:08:08.369
you'll find yourself completely busy

00:08:08.369 --> 00:08:12.171
and overwhelmed at times,
and if you just stop

00:08:12.171 --> 00:08:14.038
and look around and go wow
I'm actually doing this,

00:08:14.038 --> 00:08:16.633
you know this is kind
of what I had in mind.

00:08:16.633 --> 00:08:20.800
It's just that you get so
caught up in the whirlwind of it

00:08:22.499 --> 00:08:23.978
you don't realize that,
you got to just stop

00:08:23.978 --> 00:08:26.311
and think wow this is it, I appreciate it.

00:08:26.311 --> 00:08:27.852
I wanted to figure out
how I could remove myself

00:08:27.852 --> 00:08:29.758
from competition and still be a pro skater

00:08:29.758 --> 00:08:32.341
and that had not been done yet.

00:08:34.396 --> 00:08:36.452
Because if you're not going to compete,

00:08:36.452 --> 00:08:39.345
the magazine's not going to cover you,

00:08:39.345 --> 00:08:41.439
your sponsors are likely to drop you,

00:08:41.439 --> 00:08:43.868
and the kids who are buying your products

00:08:43.868 --> 00:08:46.118
are going to forget about you.

00:08:47.024 --> 00:08:50.804
And there was no such thing
as a video skater back then

00:08:50.804 --> 00:08:52.747
that hadn't really come into play yet.

00:08:52.747 --> 00:08:55.389
There was no YouTube, you
know, the skate videos

00:08:55.389 --> 00:08:59.556
were usually based on the
best guys in the competitions.

00:09:01.388 --> 00:09:04.600
And so I went and talked to Stacy, and--

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:05.760
- Who was you boss at the time.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.040
- Stacy Peralta was my coach,

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.420
and I actually had my brother
some with me as backup

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.122
and he approached Stacy and said,

00:09:12.122 --> 00:09:17.003
"Look, Tony's really having a
problem with the competitions.

00:09:17.003 --> 00:09:18.795
"It's kind of wrecking him.

00:09:18.795 --> 00:09:21.974
"It's ruining the fun of skating."

00:09:21.974 --> 00:09:23.450
And I told him that and so he said,

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.646
"Well, you know, I understand,

00:09:25.646 --> 00:09:27.588
"and maybe you might want to take a break,

00:09:27.588 --> 00:09:29.578
"but don't give it up completely.

00:09:29.578 --> 00:09:31.680
"'Cause you may want to come back,

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.247
"you may enjoy it, and I
don't know if we're going

00:09:35.247 --> 00:09:36.687
"to be able to sustain your career

00:09:36.687 --> 00:09:37.951
"if you're not competing."

00:09:37.951 --> 00:09:41.103
And I did take a break, I
stopped competing for a while,

00:09:41.103 --> 00:09:43.709
almost a year, maybe a little less.

00:09:43.709 --> 00:09:46.270
And when I came back
to it, I came back with

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:48.475
sort of a fresh perspective

00:09:48.475 --> 00:09:51.274
that I don't really care how it lays out.

00:09:51.274 --> 00:09:53.045
I'm going to go and do my best,

00:09:53.045 --> 00:09:56.191
and I'm going to take chances
that I maybe hadn't taken before,

00:09:56.191 --> 00:10:00.972
and if that puts me in
last place, then so be it.

00:10:00.972 --> 00:10:03.230
But if I succeed, then I'm going to be

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:05.147
on this whole other level of skating.

00:10:05.147 --> 00:10:06.529
And that's what happened.

00:10:06.529 --> 00:10:08.465
I mean, and the contest that I won then,

00:10:08.465 --> 00:10:11.048
I won by a long shot, you know,

00:10:12.302 --> 00:10:14.597
'cause I was doing stuff
that was experimental,

00:10:14.597 --> 00:10:16.576
and stuff that I wasn't
really confident with,

00:10:16.576 --> 00:10:18.914
but I could pull it through somehow.

00:10:18.914 --> 00:10:22.685
And if it didn't happen then
I didn't do well at all.

00:10:22.685 --> 00:10:24.984
And I stopped caring about
the end result so much.

